    "lossyReason": "PLAN.md S.10.2: psdexport emits layer channels in its own fixed order (0,1,2,-1; gray 0,-1) and always emits an alpha channel, because the engine model is RGBA. This fixture was authored with alpha first (-1,0,1,2), so every re-exported record's channelIds differ in order. The PSD spec does not prescribe channel order and readers key on the ID, so this is a spelling difference rather than lost data. Allowlisting a path suppresses the WHOLE assertion, so this entry on its own would also excuse a dropped -2 user mask or a corrupted ID; it does not, because the ID set is asserted separately at psdRecords[N].channelIds.preserved, which is emitted strictly and which no writer.lossy entry can reach. That path permits exactly one gained channel, the alpha psdexport always writes, and nothing else. psd-tools 1.19.0 reads the re-export correctly; ImageMagick was not installed in this environment, so this fixture rests on one independent reader rather than two (see testdata/VERIFICATION.md). PLAN.md S.10.3: psdBlendKey on the hidden `</Layer group>` bounding dividers (lsct 3) - psdexport's newGroupEndRecord hardcodes the divider's blend key to `pass`, while this fixture's writer emitted `norm`. Which one Photoshop writes is NOT established: no Photoshop-authored file is available to check against, and the spec does not prescribe it. The field is inert - readers identify the record by lsct 3 - so it is recorded rather than changed on a guess. PLAN.md S.10.4: psdimport rasterizes the layer mask into a document-sized buffer and discards the mask rectangle and its default fill, so the re-export stores a full-canvas mask.rect with defaultFill 255 instead of the original rect and fill. The mask still attenuates exactly the same pixels - the discarded default fill is folded into the rasterized mask - so only the stored extent and fill byte differ, not the coverage."
  },
